1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,571
What happens when a second module needs the same error handling and security as the first?

2
00:00:05,571 --> 00:00:09,226
We copy it, and now every fix must be made twice.

3
00:00:09,226 --> 00:00:13,761
So we extract the shared parts into our own Spring Boot starters.

4
00:00:13,761 --> 00:00:18,142
The error starter turns every failure into the same clean response.

5
00:00:18,142 --> 00:00:22,559
The security starter protects every endpoint in exactly the same way.

6
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:28,869
The OTP and Redis starters give any module one-time codes and caching with a single dependency.

7
00:00:28,869 --> 00:00:33,357
Each starter configures itself, so a new module gets all of it for free.

8
00:00:33,357 --> 00:00:38,321
Same behavior, half the code, and every fix now lands in one place.

9
00:00:38,321 --> 00:00:42,261
Next, the money rules begin, with the commission on every payment.

